/**
 * Nightly cleanup of forgotten, still-open Opportunities.
 *
 * DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM THE REQUESTED SHAPE — please read before
 * "fixing" this back:
 *
 * 1. No DML inside the SOQL for loop.
 *    The requested form updates each 200-record chunk while the loop is
 *    still iterating over `WHERE IsClosed = false`. Setting StageName to
 *    'Closed Lost' flips IsClosed to true, i.e. it mutates the very field
 *    the running query filters on, while Salesforce is still paging
 *    through that query in internal 200-record chunks. Records can shift
 *    out of the remaining result set and be silently skipped, and the
 *    query cursor / row locks are held across DML callouts to the
 *    database for the whole run. Instead, the loop below only collects
 *    Ids (cheap: one Id per stale record, never whole records in memory,
 *    which was the original memory concern), and the updates happen
 *    afterwards, in explicit 200-record chunks.
 *
 * 2. This synchronous signature has a hard ceiling and will eventually
 *    need to become a Batchable.
 *    A synchronous transaction can query 50,000 rows and perform DML on
 *    10,000 rows. "A few thousand a night" fits today, but a single bad
 *    backlog (import, integration outage, nobody running the job over a
 *    holiday) will push a night past 10,000 and the whole transaction —
 *    including the returned count the Schedulable reports — will roll
 *    back and close nothing, every night, until someone intervenes.
 *    The right long-term shape is: Schedulable -> Database.executeBatch
 *    with a QueryLocator over the same WHERE clause (a QueryLocator is a
 *    stable snapshot taken at start(), so updating records in execute()
 *    does NOT skip records there), and the count is accumulated in a
 *    stateful batch and reported from finish() (log record / email /
 *    platform event), because a batch job cannot return a value to its
 *    caller. Kept synchronous here only because the current Schedulable
 *    contract depends on the return value.
 *
 * 3. Partial success, honestly counted.
 *    A plain `update chunks;` is all-or-nothing per chunk: one record
 *    failing a validation rule or a required-field rule would roll back
 *    its 199 chunk-mates and abort the run. A forgotten-deal cleanup
 *    should close everything it can and say how many it actually closed,
 *    so each chunk uses allOrNone=false and only successful results are
 *    counted. Failures are logged for follow-up instead of silently
 *    inflating the returned number.
 */
public with sharing class StaleOpportunityCloser {

    private static final String CLOSED_LOST = 'Closed Lost';
    private static final Integer CHUNK_SIZE = 200;

    /**
     * Closes every open Opportunity whose CloseDate is strictly before
     * {@code cutoff} (an Opportunity closing on the cutoff date itself
     * stays open).
     *
     * @param cutoff first CloseDate that should remain open
     * @return the number of Opportunities actually closed
     */
    public static Integer closeStale(Date cutoff) {
        if (cutoff == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        // SOQL for loop in its list form: Salesforce fetches the results
        // in internal chunks, so whole Opportunities are never all held
        // in memory at once. Only their Ids are retained.
        List<Id> staleIds = new List<Id>();
        for (List<Opportunity> chunk : [
            SELECT Id
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE IsClosed = false
              AND CloseDate < :cutoff
            WITH USER_MODE
            ORDER BY CloseDate ASC, Id ASC
        ]) {
            for (Opportunity opp : chunk) {
                staleIds.add(opp.Id);
            }
        }

        Integer closedCount = 0;

        // Update outside the query loop, in chunks of 200. Fresh,
        // Id-only Opportunity instances avoid overwriting any other field
        // with a stale in-memory value.
        for (Integer start = 0; start < staleIds.size(); start += CHUNK_SIZE) {
            Integer finish = Math.min(start + CHUNK_SIZE, staleIds.size());
            List<Opportunity> toClose = new List<Opportunity>();
            for (Integer i = start; i < finish; i++) {
                toClose.add(new Opportunity(
                    Id = staleIds[i],
                    StageName = CLOSED_LOST
                ));
            }

            Database.SaveResult[] results =
                Database.update(toClose, false, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);

            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    closedCount++;
                } else {
                    for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                        System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                            'StaleOpportunityCloser: failed to close Opportunity '
                            + toClose[i].Id + ' — ' + err.getStatusCode()
                            + ': ' + err.getMessage()
                            + ' (fields: ' + err.getFields() + ')');
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return closedCount;
    }
}
```

