/**
 * Creates follow-up Tasks for Opportunities from the weekly pipeline review.
 *
 * DEVIATION FROM THE REQUESTED IMPLEMENTATION, AND WHY:
 * The requested approach — inserting each Task individually in a loop inside
 * try/catch — was NOT used. Apex allows at most 150 DML statements per
 * transaction, and this method is routinely called with 150-250 Opportunity
 * Ids in a single call. A per-record insert would therefore throw a
 * LimitException part-way through the batch (at the 151st Task), aborting the
 * remaining records regardless of the try/catch (governor limit exceptions
 * cannot be caught and rolled back selectively), and would be wasteful even
 * below the limit.
 *
 * Instead, all Tasks are created with a single bulkified
 * Database.insert(tasks, false) call (allOrNone = false). This gives exactly
 * the required behaviour with one DML statement: records rejected by the
 * compliance validation rules fail individually without preventing the other
 * Tasks from being committed, and each Database.SaveResult identifies which
 * record failed so its Opportunity Id can be returned to the caller.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /**
     * Creates one follow-up Task per Opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the Opportunities to follow up on.
     * @return Ids of the Opportunities whose Task could not be created
     *         (including Ids that do not resolve to an Opportunity);
     *         an empty Set when every Task was created successfully.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate and drop nulls so each Opportunity gets exactly one Task.
        Set<Id> uniqueOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                uniqueOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }
        if (uniqueOpportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [
                SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
                FROM Opportunity
                WHERE Id IN :uniqueOpportunityIds
            ]
        );

        // Any supplied Id that is not a visible Opportunity cannot get a Task.
        for (Id opportunityId : uniqueOpportunityIds) {
            if (!opportunitiesById.containsKey(opportunityId)) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }

        // Build all Tasks up front, keeping a parallel list that maps each
        // Task (by index) back to its Opportunity for failure reporting.
        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();
        List<Id> taskOpportunityIds = new List<Id>();
        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(7);

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunitiesById.values()) {
            tasksToInsert.add(
                new Task(
                    WhatId = opp.Id,
                    OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                    Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                    ActivityDate = dueDate,
                    Status = 'Not Started',
                    Priority = 'Normal'
                )
            );
            taskOpportunityIds.add(opp.Id);
        }

        if (tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // Single bulk DML statement with partial success enabled: a Task
        // rejected by a validation rule (or any other record-level error)
        // fails on its own while the remaining Tasks are still committed.
        Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.insert(tasksToInsert, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(taskOpportunityIds[i]);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

**What I did differently, and why**

* **One bulk `@future` call, not one per Opportunity:** Calling `AccountRatingService.recalculate(opp.AccountId)` per record in a 200-record CPQ batch would exceed the limit of 50 future calls per transaction. I collect and deduplicate all affected `AccountId`s and pass a `Set<Id>` — an allowed `@future` parameter type — in a single call.
* **Also recalculated on `AccountId` change, delete, and undelete, including the old account:** Otherwise, reparenting or deleting the last open Opportunity would leave a stale `Rating` on the former account.
* **Async-context guard:** `@future` cannot be called from future, batch, or Queueable contexts. If the trigger already runs asynchronously, the same logic runs synchronously instead of failing.
* **Only relevant updates and only changed accounts:** Updates that change neither `StageName`, `Amount`, nor `AccountId` do not enqueue work, and accounts whose `Rating` is already correct are not updated.

