```apex
/**
 * Matches Leads to existing Contacts by email.
 *
 * Why not a nested loop:
 * The nested-loop approach is O(leads * contacts). At the stated maximum
 * of 3,000 x 3,000 that is up to 9,000,000 case-insensitive string
 * comparisons, each also trimming both values. Apex has a CPU-time
 * governor limit (10s synchronous) shared with the rest of the import
 * transaction (validation, triggers, flows, DML), so that quadratic cost
 * risks CPU timeouts and scales badly if volumes grow.
 *
 * Building a Map keyed by normalized email is O(leads + contacts):
 * one pass over the contacts, then one O(1) lookup per lead. It is
 * equally readable, preserves "first contact wins", and performs no
 * SOQL or DML, as required.
 */
public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> results = new List<Id>();

        if (leads == null) {
            return results;
        }

        // Index contacts by normalized email. First contact in list order
        // wins, so an existing key is never overwritten.
        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact contactRecord : contacts) {
                if (contactRecord == null || String.isBlank(contactRecord.Email)) {
                    continue;
                }
                String normalizedEmail = contactRecord.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
                if (!contactIdByEmail.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                    contactIdByEmail.put(normalizedEmail, contactRecord.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // One lookup per lead, preserving input size and order.
        // Map.get() returns null when there is no match, as required.
        for (Lead leadRecord : leads) {
            if (leadRecord == null || String.isBlank(leadRecord.Email)) {
                results.add(null);
                continue;
            }
            String normalizedEmail = leadRecord.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
            results.add(contactIdByEmail.get(normalizedEmail));
        }

        return results;
    }
}
```

**Change explained:** The requested one `enqueueJob` per case cannot be implemented as-is. Salesforce allows only **50 Queueable jobs per transaction**, so the nightly bulk close of up to 200 cases would fail part-way with a `LimitException`, losing surveys. Instead the trigger collects all newly-closed case Ids and enqueues **one** job for the whole transaction (still one job per trigger chunk of ≤200, well within limits). The original isolation intent is preserved differently: the job builds all Tasks and inserts them with `allOrNone = false` in `USER_MODE`, so one bad Task cannot roll back the others, and it re-validates `IsClosed`/`ContactId` and re-queries `CaseNumber` at execution time (state may change between trigger and async run). A single-Id constructor is retained for convenience/one-off use.

